<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · sea</p><h1 class="enc-title">Strait of Malacca</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Malacca Strait</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Strait of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malacca">Malacca</a> is the channel between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malay-peninsula">Malay Peninsula</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, one of the busiest sea routes in the world. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/srivijaya">Srivijaya</a> controlled it, taxing trade between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit Empire</a> note the later rise of Malacca.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/strait-of-malacca.jpg" alt="Strait of Malacca" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Derives from png uploaded by Wolrd blank map · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Strait_of_Malacca_highlighted.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Aceh grew up at the northern tip of Sumatra, where the <b>Strait of Malacca</b> opens onto the Indian Ocean, an area earlier dominated by the port-kingdom of Lamuri and by Samudera Pasai, one of the first Muslim states of Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aceh-sultanate">Aceh Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Anglo-Dutch Treaty of 1824 settled the division of the region: the Dutch ceded Malacca and their Indian posts and accepted British possession of Singapore, while Britain gave up Bencoolen and its claims in Sumatra, leaving the archipelago south of the <b>Strait of Malacca</b> to the Dutch.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-empire">Dutch Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The new state claimed Malacca's royal line, its regalia and court traditions, and its authority over the Malay lands around the southern <b>Strait of Malacca</b>: the Johor River basin, the islands of the Riau archipelago, parts of the east coast of Sumatra and, at times, Pahang.</blockquote><a href="/empire/johor-sultanate">Johor Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Strait of Malacca</b> had been dominated by Srivijaya, centred on Palembang, until the Cholas weakened it in the 11th century, and by the end of the 13th by the Javanese Singhasari and Majapahit.</blockquote><a href="/empire/malacca-sultanate">Malacca Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…Asia from the seventh to the thirteenth centuries, a thalassocracy built not on territorial conquest and agricultural surplus but on control of the sea lanes and the extraction of revenue from the traffic passing through the <b>Strait of Malacca</b> and the waters around Sumatra and the Malay Peninsula.</blockquote><a href="/empire/srivijaya-empire">Srivijaya Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the Malacca Sultanate (1402–1511), a hub of international trade and Islamic learning on the <b>Strait of Malacca</b>, Classical Malay became the lingua franca of the region.</blockquote><a href="/language/malay">Malay · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li></ul></section>
